<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter13_overriding_and_virtual_functions">
    <title>Overriding and Virtual Functions</title>

    <p>
        A derived class can provide a new implementation of a member function that
        it inherits from a base class. This is called <term>overriding</term>. The
        base function must be virtual for a call through a base reference or pointer
        to select the derived implementation at run time.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_virtual_member_functions">
        <title>Virtual Member Functions</title>

        <p>
            The keyword <c>virtual</c> tells C++ that a derived class may replace a
            member function. The derived declaration should use <c>override</c> to
            state explicitly that it is intended to override a base function.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_overriding_report_role">
            <title>A derived class overrides a virtual member function</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_overriding_report_role"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Employee {
public:
    virtual void report_role() const {
        std::cout << "Employee\n";
    }

    virtual ~Employee() = default;
};

class HourlyEmployee : public Employee {
public:
    void report_role() const override {
        std::cout << "Hourly employee\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    HourlyEmployee employee;
    Employee& employee_reference = employee;

    employee_reference.report_role();

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The reference has the static type <c>Employee&amp;</c>, but it refers to an
            <c>HourlyEmployee</c> object. Because <c>report_role()</c> is virtual,
            the call uses the derived implementation.
        </p>

        <p>
            The <c>override</c> keyword is valuable because the compiler checks that
            the derived declaration really matches a virtual base function. A typo,
            different parameter list, or missing <c>const</c> qualifier then becomes
            a compiler error instead of an accidental new function.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_override_and_final">
        <title><c>override</c> and <c>final</c></title>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter13_override_and_final">
            <title>Keywords for controlling inherited behavior</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="22%"/>
                <col width="39%"/>
                <col width="39%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Keyword</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Where it appears</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Purpose</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p><c>virtual</c></p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Base-class member function</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Allows a derived class to provide a run-time replacement.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p><c>override</c></p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Derived member function</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Requests a compiler check that a base virtual function is being overridden.</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p><c>final</c></p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Overriding member or class</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Prevents further overriding or further derivation.</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            A member function declared with <c>final</c> cannot be overridden by a
            later derived class. A class declared with <c>final</c> cannot be used as
            a base class. These restrictions can protect a design when further
            customization would be unsafe or misleading.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter13_final_member_and_class"
                 language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
class Report {
public:
    virtual void print() const final {
        // This implementation cannot be overridden.
    }
};

class SecureReport final : public Report {
    // No class may derive from SecureReport.
};

int main() {
    SecureReport report;
    report.print();
    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_function_hiding">
        <title>Overriding and Function Hiding</title>

        <p>
            A derived declaration can hide all base functions with the same name,
            even when the parameter lists differ. The <c>using</c> declaration can
            bring the hidden base overloads into the derived class's overload set.
        </p>

        <p>
            Hiding is based on the function's name. It does not require the base
            function to be virtual, and it does not mean that the derived function
            replaces the base implementation for polymorphic calls. This makes
            hiding different from overriding.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter13_function_hiding"
                 language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Printer {
public:
    void print() const {
        std::cout << "No argument\n";
    }

    void print(int copies) const {
        std::cout << copies << " copies\n";
    }
};

class ColorPrinter : public Printer {
public:
    using Printer::print;

    void print() const {
        std::cout << "Color output\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    ColorPrinter printer;
    printer.print();
    printer.print(2);

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            Without <c>using Printer::print;</c>, the declaration of
            <c>ColorPrinter::print()</c> would hide the inherited overload
            <c>Printer::print(int)</c> when called through a <c>ColorPrinter</c>
            object. The <c>using</c> declaration makes both overloads available.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_comparing_overriding_hiding_overloading">
        <title>Overriding, Hiding, and Overloading Compared</title>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter13_overriding_hiding_overloading">
            <title>Three similar but different ideas</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="22%"/>
                <col width="28%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Idea</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>What must match?</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>When is it selected?</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Useful tool</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Overriding</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>A virtual base function's name, parameters, and relevant qualifiers</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>At run time through a base reference or pointer</p></cell>
                    <cell><p><c>override</c></p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Hiding</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Only the function name needs to be declared in the derived class</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>During name lookup</p></cell>
                    <cell><p><c>using Base::function_name</c></p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Overloading</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Same name in one scope, with different parameter lists</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>At compile time from the argument types</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Function overload resolution</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The <c>const</c> qualifier is part of the relevant member-function
            signature for overriding. If a base function is declared
            <c>void display() const</c>, a derived function declared
            <c>void display()</c> does not override it because the qualifiers differ.
            Writing <c>override</c> makes the compiler identify this mistake.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_overriding_hiding_overloading">
            <title>Overriding and restoring hidden overloads</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_overriding_hiding_overloading"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class BasePrinter {
public:
    virtual void report(int value) const {
        std::cout << "Base report: " << value << '\n';
    }

    void print() const {
        std::cout << "Base print\n";
    }

    void print(int copies) const {
        std::cout << copies << " copies\n";
    }
};

class ColorPrinter : public BasePrinter {
public:
    void report(int value) const override {
        std::cout << "Color report: " << value << '\n';
    }

    using BasePrinter::print;

    void print(double brightness) const {
        std::cout << "Brightness: " << brightness << '\n';
    }
};

int main() {
    ColorPrinter printer;
    BasePrinter& printer_reference = printer;

    printer_reference.report(7);
    printer.print();
    printer.print(2);
    printer.print(0.8);

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            <c>report(int)</c> overrides the virtual base function. The derived
            <c>print(double)</c> would hide the two base <c>print</c> overloads, but
            <c>using BasePrinter::print;</c> makes those base overloads available
            again. The calls are therefore resolved for three different argument
            forms.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_overriding_visualization">
        <title>Visualizing an Overridden Function</title>

        <p>
            The visualization shows an <c>Employee</c> reference referring to an
            <c>HourlyEmployee</c> object and calling the overridden function.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter13_overriding"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aclass%20Employee%20%7B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20virtual%20void%20report_role%28%29%20const%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Employee%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20virtual%20~Employee%28%29%20%3D%20default%3B%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aclass%20HourlyEmployee%20%3A%20public%20Employee%20%7B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20void%20report_role%28%29%20const%20override%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Hourly%20employee%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20HourlyEmployee%20employee%3B%0A%20%20%20%20Employee%26%20employee_reference%20%3D%20employee%3B%0A%20%20%20%20employee_reference.report_role%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=390&amp;codeDivWidth=420&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="16:10">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises_chapter13_overriding">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_overriding_1">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What does the <c>override</c> keyword ask the compiler to check?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>That the function is private.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p><c>override</c> concerns inherited virtual functions, not access.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>That the derived function matches a virtual function in a base class.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The compiler checks the intended overriding relationship.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>That no object can call the function.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p><c>override</c> does not prevent calls.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>That the function is automatically static.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Overridden functions are associated with object behavior.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_overriding_multiple">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which statements about <c>virtual</c>, <c>override</c>, and
                    <c>final</c> are correct? Select all that apply.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p><c>override</c> helps detect a mismatch with a base virtual function.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The compiler checks the intended override.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p><c>final</c> always makes a function private.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p><c>final</c> controls further overriding, not access.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A virtual base function can be replaced by a derived implementation.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>This is the purpose of virtual dispatch.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A class declared <c>final</c> cannot be used as a base class.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p><c>final</c> can prevent further class derivation.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_overriding_hiding_multiple">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which statements correctly distinguish overriding from hiding?
                    Select all that apply.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>Overriding requires a matching virtual base function.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>A derived function uses the base virtual interface when it overrides.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>Hiding can occur when a derived function has the same name but different parameters.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Name lookup can hide all base overloads with that name.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Hiding automatically causes dynamic dispatch through a base reference.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Dynamic dispatch is associated with virtual overriding, not ordinary name hiding.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>A <c>using Base::function_name;</c> declaration can restore hidden base overloads.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The using declaration brings the base overload set into the derived scope.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_overriding_2">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Why might a derived class use <c>using Printer::print;</c>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    It brings hidden base-class overloads named <c>print</c> into the
                    derived class's overload set.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_overriding_parsons"
                  number="yes"
                  adaptive="yes"
                  indentation="show">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Arrange the blocks to override <c>report_role()</c> correctly.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <blocks randomize="yes">
                <block order="1">
                    <cline>class HourlyEmployee : public Employee {</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="2">
                    <cline>public:</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="3">
                    <cline>    void report_role() const override {</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="4">
                    <cline>        std::cout &lt;&lt; "Hourly employee\n";</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="5">
                    <cline>    }</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="6">
                    <cline>};</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="3" correct="no">
                    <cline>    void report_role(int level) const override {</cline>
                </block>
            </blocks>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>
